<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free State of Bavaria</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – present · capital Munich</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Bavaria as a republic, from the 1918 revolution through the Nazi era to a prosperous and self-confident state of modern Germany.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On 7 November 1918 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/independent-social-democratic-party-of-germany">Independent Socialist</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurt-eisner">Kurt Eisner</a> proclaimed the Free State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, ending the Wittelsbach monarchy. Eisner was assassinated in February 1919, and a short-lived <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bavarian-soviet-republic">Bavarian Soviet Republic</a> was crushed that spring. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a> constitution of 1919 established Bavaria as a parliamentary free state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Inter-war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> became a conservative stronghold and refuge of the extreme right, where Hitler's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beer-hall-putsch">Beer Hall Putsch</a> failed in 1923. After 1933 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazis</a> took control and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> became the capital of the movement. After 1945 Bavaria was in the American zone; its 1946 constitution created a state long dominated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-social-union-in-bavaria">Christian Social Union</a>, which built a high-technology economy.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federal-republic-of-germany">Federal Republic of Germany</a> in 1949, though its Landtag rejected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basic-law-for-the-federal-republic-of-germany">Basic Law</a> while accepting its validity. It remains one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany's</a> sixteen states, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> as capital.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-state-of-bavaria.jpg" alt="Free State of Bavaria" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: diese Datei: Jwnabd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bavaria_(lozengy).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Munich</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kurt Eisner, Franz Josef Strauss</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Bavarian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>People's State of Bavaria, Freistaat Bayern</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1972&amp;focus=free-state-of-bavaria" aria-label="Free State of Bavaria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4949 493 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free State of Bavaria lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5269 749L5269 749M5269 749L5269 749M5269 749L5300 622M5269 749L5300 622M5269 749L5227 665M5269 749L5251 687"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5269 749h0M5269 749h0M5300 622h0M5300 622h0M5227 665h0M5251 687h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5269 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5269 749h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Munich. <a href="/atlas?year=1972&amp;focus=free-state-of-bavaria">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
